  [1mWhat changed:[0m
  - [1m[38;5;153mauth.js[39m:[0m a new [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrServiceToken[39m middleware. It lets a request through if it sends [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
    [38;5;153m<token>[39m matching the [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m environment variable; otherwise it falls back to the existing login
    check. If that variable isn't set, token access is turned off.
  - [1m[38;5;153mserver.js[39m:[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now uses the new middleware. No other routes changed.

  [1mTesting:[0m I started the server on a free port and sent requests by hand (there are no automated tests in this repo):

  ┌────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐
  │        Request         │        Result         │
  ├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ No credentials         │ 401                   │
  ├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ Wrong token            │ 401                   │
  ├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ Correct token          │ 200, returns the data │
  ├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ Session cookie         │ 200, same as before   │
  ├────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ Token variable not set │ 401                   │
  └────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘

  My first test run hit a port another process was already using, so I threw those results away; the table is from the
  second run.

  [1mTo finish setup:[0m
  1. Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m on the server to a long random value, e.g. from [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m.
  2. Give that value to the dashboard team and have their script send it as [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m.

  [1mStill open:[0m [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m accepts any cookie named [38;5;153msession=[39m and never verifies it, so anyone can still get in with a
  made-up cookie. Fixing that is a separate change, but this route is only as secure as that check.

  I haven't committed anything.

